story=[]
story += [Spacer(1,2*mm),P('DIAGNOBOT','titlex'),HRFlowable(width='38%',thickness=2,color=teal,hAlign='CENTER'),Spacer(1,4*mm)]
story += [P('Executive Summary','h1x'),P('DiagnoBot is a student-built prototype for image-assisted health triage. The software combines a fine-tuned MobileNetV3-Small classifier for ten skin-image folder labels, Moondream via Ollama for image descriptions, Llama 3.2 via Ollama for short text summaries, and deterministic Python rules for symptom extraction and urgency cues. The broader codebase also contains a TorchXRayVision DenseNet-121 chest X-ray model path. The latest skin model was trained on 932 images and achieved 70.7% accuracy, 70.3% balanced accuracy, and 0.699 macro-F1 on a 140-image internal test split. The test split is image-level, not patient-level, and there is no external test cohort; these results are preliminary and do not establish clinical performance.'),P('The physical concept uses an available Raspberry Pi 5, ESP32, L298N, chassis, motors, and planned MAX30102, MLX90614, and USB webcam. The repository does not yet implement Pi GPIO, ESP32 serial, sensor reads, or motor control. This report separates working software from the proposed hardware build and provides recent paper references for the literature review.','bx'),P('Report contents','h2x'),P('1 Project overview | 2 Problem statement and objectives | 3 Literature review | 4 Proposed solution and models | 5 Working principle | 6 Methodology | 7 Prototype and hardware | 8 Evaluation metrics and results | 9 Limitations and recommendations | 10 Conclusion | References','leftx'),P('1. Project Overview','h1x'),P('The project aims to demonstrate how image analysis, basic physiological sensing, local AI models, and a human-facing interface could be combined in a low-cost health-triage robot. The current repository is primarily a FastAPI web application with dashboard pages and ML pipeline modules. It has a locally stored ten-class skin checkpoint, a separate X-ray route, local vision/text model paths, and a deterministic triage layer.'),P('1.1 Current software modules','h2x')]
mods=[['Module','Current role'],['Skin image classifier','MobileNetV3-Small returns one of ten dataset labels and prediction confidence.'],['Vision-language model','Moondream describes image content; it is not the skin classifier.'],['Clinical text model','Llama 3.2 summarizes supplied context; no answer-accuracy benchmark exists.'],['Chest X-ray path','TorchXRayVision DenseNet-121 with MIMIC-Ch weights; not evaluated in this report.'],['Rules','Python extraction and urgency cues; deterministic, not learned.'],['Web service','FastAPI endpoints and dashboards for image uploads, reports, and triage.']]
story += [tbl([[P(x,'thx') for x in mods[0]]]+[[P(x,'smx') for x in r] for r in mods[1:]],[47*mm,CW-47*mm]),Spacer(1,5),P('The app defaults to MOCK_MODE=true, so model-backed calls may be replaced by simulation/fallback behavior. When enabled, the LLM/VLM use Ollama tags `llama3.2` and `moondream`. These are tags, not pinned versions; record model digests for reproducibility.'),P('2. Problem Statement and Objectives','h1x'),P('2.1 Problem statement','h2x'),P('The system is intended to collect a skin image and basic vital signs, process them locally, and present an understandable summary for human review. The current software and physical prototype are not fully integrated: the dataset provenance and labels are not adequately documented, the MAX30102/MLX90614/webcam are planned purchases, and the code repository has no Pi/ESP32 sensor or motor-control implementation. The classifier has only 140 internal test images and no patient/source identifiers, so related images could occur across partitions.'),P('2.2 Project objectives','h2x')]
for x in ['Provide a local user interface and API for skin-image input and decision-support output.','Train and evaluate a compact ten-class MobileNetV3-Small skin classifier.','Describe separate roles for the skin classifier, local LLM/VLMs, and deterministic triage rules.','Plan sensor, camera, display, and chassis integration using the components available to the team.','Report measurable results and state limits before claiming medical usefulness.']:story.append(bullet(x))
story += [P('2.3 Scope','h2x'),P('Included: software architecture, skin-model training/evaluation, local model roles, hardware plan, and student-prototype demonstration. Not yet demonstrated: clinical diagnosis, external validation, live sensor acquisition, Pi inference performance, motor control, or end-to-end robot testing.','callx'),P('3. Literature Review - Recent Papers','h1x'),P('The selected 2024-2025 studies cover data efficiency, label noise, fairness, model fusion, clinical data documentation, embedded sensing, and reporting standards. Their numerical results use different datasets and test protocols; they are context, not direct competitors to DiagnoBot.')]
story += [P('<b>[1] Chen, Liu, and Yang (2024) - CDD-Net and Derm104.</b> This few-shot classification study introduced 4,483 clinical images across 104 categories and a multiscale feature-fusion module. It reported gains up to 9.14 percentage points for selected few-shot settings. Relevance: small labeled datasets are common in dermatology; however, category-holdout few-shot evaluation differs from DiagnoBot\'s ordinary ten-class image split.'),P('<b>[2] Shen et al. (2024) - learning from online community images.</b> The authors used contrastive pretraining and label filtering for 22 skin diseases. Reported top-1 accuracy increased from 42.05% to 45.05% after pretraining and reached 49.64% after filtering with a larger validation set. Relevance: correcting noisy labels can matter as much as architecture changes. Internet images can introduce source and population bias.'),P('<b>[3] Dip et al. (2024) - equitable skin prediction.</b> This transfer-learning and domain-adaptation study evaluated Diverse Dermatology Images, which includes underrepresented and common skin tones. It highlights that average accuracy can mask subgroup performance gaps. DiagnoBot has no skin-tone metadata or subgroup evaluation, so representative data and subgroup checks are future requirements.'),P('<b>[4] Alruwaili and Mohamed (2025) - multi-architecture classification.</b> The study combined EfficientNet-B0, EfficientNet-B2, and ResNet50 on 27,153 Kaggle images and reported 99.14% accuracy using an 80/10/10 split. The result is not directly comparable to DiagnoBot: data scale, labels, and split differ, and image-level random splits may inflate results when related images overlap. It illustrates why accuracy must be interpreted with dataset and split details.'),P('<b>[5] Yan et al. (2025) - PanDerm.</b> This dermatology foundation model used over two million multimodal images from 11 institutions and four imaging modalities, with broad benchmark and reader studies. It is far larger than a student model and is not a direct comparator. Relevance: diverse datasets, multiple modalities, and clinician-reader evaluation strengthen medical AI evidence.'),P('<b>[6] Sultana et al. (2025) - SkinDisNet dataset.</b> The paper describes 1,710 smartphone images from hospitals in Bangladesh across six conditions, with clinical/demographic metadata. Labels differ from DiagnoBot, but its provenance and metadata documentation demonstrate the value of traceable data.'),P('<b>[7] Pawar et al. (2024) - ESP32 health-monitoring prototype.</b> This conference prototype combines an ESP32, MAX30102, temperature sensor, display, and connected app. It is relevant to the embedded-sensing plan; it does not validate DiagnoBot or demonstrate clinical-grade readings.'),P('<b>[8] Tejani et al. (2024) - CLAIM 2024 reporting guidance.</b> The checklist recommends transparent reporting of data source, partition sizes and split unit, preprocessing, evaluation, and external testing. It distinguishes internal held-out testing from a separate external dataset and generally recommends disjoint patient-level or higher partitions.'),P('3.1 Main lessons for DiagnoBot','h2x')]
for x in ['Improve label quality and document image provenance before changing the model architecture.','Use patient/source-grouped splits and independent external testing when possible.','Measure performance by class and relevant subgroup; aggregate accuracy alone is insufficient.','Evaluate sensors, generated language, rules, and the image classifier as separate subsystems.']:story.append(bullet(x))
story += [P('4. Proposed Solution and Model Selection','h1x'),P('The proposed solution combines the current local application with a planned robot front end. The camera supplies images; the skin classifier provides class probabilities; a vision-language model generates a separate observation; a language model summarizes supplied context; deterministic rules extract warning signals; and the display presents each output for human review. No generated response should be presented as a confirmed diagnosis.'),P('4.1 Exact model inventory and rationale','h2x')]
model_rows=[['Component','Model used in code','Why / intended role'],['Skin classification','Torchvision MobileNetV3-Small, ImageNet default weights, fine-tuned in PyTorch','Compact transfer-learning model for CPU/edge experiments; ten outputs correspond to local folder names.'],['Image description','Moondream via local Ollama tag `moondream`','Generates text observations about the image; separate from classification and not evaluated here.'],['Clinical summary','Llama 3.2 via local Ollama tag `llama3.2`','Generates concise context summaries; no project answer-quality metric is implemented.'],['Chest X-ray','TorchXRayVision DenseNet-121, `densenet121-res224-mimic_ch`','Separate pretrained chest X-ray path in the larger codebase; outside skin-model evaluation.'],['Extraction/triage','Project Python rules','Deterministic and inspectable; not an LLM, but not clinically validated.']]
story += [tbl([[P(v,'thx') for v in model_rows[0]]]+[[P(v,'smx') for v in row] for row in model_rows[1:]],[36*mm,69*mm,CW-105*mm]),P('4.2 Proposed architecture','h2x'),P('The software workflow and proposed physical robot connections are shown separately for clarity.','leftx'),P('Software workflow','h2x'),Diagram(),P('Figure 1. Current software path: image input, classifier, language-model paths, rules, dashboard, and human review.','capx'),HardwareDiagram(),P('Figure 2. Planned hardware links; sensor acquisition and motor-control software are not yet implemented.','capx'),P('5. Working Principle','h1x'),P('Software workflow: (1) user uploads or captures an image; (2) the image is resized to 224 x 224 and passed to MobileNetV3-Small; (3) class scores and confidence are formatted for the dashboard; (4) Moondream can provide a separate image description; (5) symptom/vital information is parsed by deterministic rules; (6) Llama 3.2 can summarize the supplied context; and (7) the user or clinician reviews the findings. The LLM output does not replace classifier evidence or a clinician.'),P('Planned robot: ESP32 reads MAX30102 and MLX90614 over I2C/SMBus and sends timestamped values by serial to the Pi. The Pi runs the service and reads the USB webcam. L298N drives motors from a separate supply. Stop motors for readings.','bx'),P('6. Methodology','h1x'),P('6.1 Dataset and split','h2x'),P('The local folder-organized dataset contains 932 images and ten labels. A seeded (42), stratified image-level split assigned 652 training images, 140 tuning/validation images, and 140 internal test images. Patient/source IDs were unavailable. Dataset origin, license, clinical reference standard, annotator qualifications, and label adjudication are not documented in the repository and must be verified before formal dissemination.'),P('Table 1. Image counts by class','h2x')]
